  "reasoning": "The patient presents with fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, weakness, fatigue, and difficulty concentrating. These are classic systemic and local signs of an acute viral upper respiratory infection. Step-by-step evaluation of the possible diagnoses reveals: 1) Allergy typically causes sneezing, rhinorrhea, and itchy/watery eyes but does not produce a fever, ruling it out. 2) Dengue is characterized by severe joint/muscle pain, rash, and sometimes bleeding, which are completely absent here. 3) Drug reactions usually involve cutaneous manifestations like rashes or itching, along with tremors or dizziness, none of which are reported. 4) Other conditions like typhoid or malaria present with prominent gastrointestinal symptoms or cyclical chills/sweats, respectively, which do not match the current presentation. The combination of fever, headache, swollen lymph nodes, and profound fatigue aligns precisely with the clinical profile of a common cold, as corroborated by similar training references that link these exact symptoms to this diagnosis.",
  "final_answer": "[DIAGNOSIS]common cold[/DIAGNOSIS]"
